  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On September 5, the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps launched ballistic missiles at a US aircraft carrier and a guided-missile destroyer in the Persian Gulf; both vessels evaded with no reported US casualties. United States Central Command responded by striking three Iranian crude oil tankers near Kharg Island and in the Gulf of Oman, permanently disabling them (CNN Sep 5; PBS Sep 5). On September 6, the IRGC claimed attacks on three oil tankers and three US-affiliated ships in the Strait of Hormuz as retaliation for the CENTCOM tanker strikes (Jerusalem Post Sep 6; Gulf News Sep 6) — CENTCOM had not confirmed the Iranian Sep 6 claims as of September 7. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>The diplomatic track reached its lowest point since the April 8 ceasefire. The June 17 Memorandum of Understanding between the United States and Iran expired unextended on August 17. President Trump on September 2 stated he had set aside any deal, warning that Iran would face far stronger military action if it retaliates again (IranWarLive; House of Commons Library briefing). Foreign Minister Araghchi, between August 15–19, stated publicly that Tehran has made "no decision to resume negotiations with Washington" and that Iran "rejects ceasefire" and demands "a complete end to the war" — not a suspension (CDM Press Aug 15; Middle East Monitor Aug 19). → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>September 6 marked the resolution horizon for F2 (Iran ceasefire announcement by Sep 6). Iranian President Pezeshkian on September 1 stated Tehran is "prepared to reciprocate" if the United States returns to the June ceasefire conditions (Time Sep 1). That statement was conditional — per the pre-declared ambiguity rule, conditional or preconditioned offers resolve as NO absent US acceptance. The US government did not accept the condition. No unconditional public Iranian commitment to resuming talks was observed within the resolution window. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Qatar's Prime Minister Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman visited Tehran in late August; Pakistan Army Chief General Asim Munir met President Pezeshkian and Parliament Speaker Ghalibaf on August 24; Oman's Foreign Minister Badr Albusaidi met FM Araghchi to discuss the Strait of Hormuz (Iran International Sep 4; Al Jazeera Aug 27). These three sets of high-level mediator visits represent an active mediation infrastructure. The visits have not produced a convened Iran-US session — both Washington (US envoy contacts halted Aug 18) and Tehran (FM explicit rejection Aug 19) have made no formal move toward a bilateral meeting. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>AIS-tracked commercial transits through the Strait of Hormuz on September 6 totalled four vessels — two tankers, a bulk carrier, and a liquid-service vessel — all travelling with transponders disabled, indicating clandestine passage rather than commercial recovery (Straits.live Day 190). The rolling seven-day average was approximately 12–13 vessels per day through September 1 (Lloyd's List Intelligence via UANI Sep 1) and has deteriorated further following September 5-6 exchanges. Iran's rial fell to an all-time low of 2.25 million per US dollar on the informal market by early September, against the Central Bank's official rate of 1.5 million, as oil export revenues remain near-zero (RFE/RL Sep 2). → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP (computed summary) ========== -->
