      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation (68%, +3pp from v48):</strong> F6 at 65% (continued IRGC attacks likely within 72h), F3 at 9% (no diplomatic off-ramp), F4 at 11% (Hormuz traffic still near-zero) — all three forecasts align against de-escalation; the tanker war exchange of Sep 5-6 has tightened the escalation-protracted distinction.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted conflict (25%, −3pp from v48):</strong> Mediator infrastructure (Qatar, Oman, Pakistan) remains active but neither side has moved toward a convened session; the protracted scenario becomes harder to distinguish from managed escalation as kinetic exchanges recur without diplomatic punctuation.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation / deal (7%, unchanged from v48):</strong> F3 at 9% and F2 resolved-NO confirm there is no active diplomatic channel; reaching de-escalation within the current forecast window requires both a US envoy contact restart and an Iranian MFA reversal from Araghchi's Aug 19 explicit rejection — neither is observable on any near-term tracker.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Tasnim framed the September 5 IRGC ballistic-missile launches against US warships as a defensive response to "illegal US naval presence in Iranian maritime space." Press TV carried IRGC naval commander interviews describing the operations as protection of "Iranian sovereignty in the Persian Gulf." No state media outlet characterized the Sep 5-6 exchanges as escalatory or a departure from prior posture — the framing was entirely defensive and reactive. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>

      <p>The Qatar PM, Pakistan army chief, and Oman FM visits to Tehran in late August received prominent placement on IRNA and Tasnim. State media framing depicted these visits as evidence of Iran's active diplomacy, not as external pressure. FM Araghchi's public statements — that Iran demands "a complete end to the war" rather than a ceasefire — were carried by IRNA without commentary characterising them as maximalist; they were presented as Iran's stated legal position. No state media outlet reported or anticipated a scheduled negotiating session between Iranian and US envoys. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan daily editorials framed the rial's fall to 2.25 million per dollar as the result of "US economic terrorism," not domestic monetary policy failure. IRNA reported GDP contraction and inflation figures without attribution to government decisions. State media gave no coverage to street-level hardship beyond framing it as a consequence of "enemy siege warfare." The domestic-narrative focus on economic siege hardened rather than acknowledged vulnerability. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

